00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.640
and give you a high level
understanding of what

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.020
Power BI is.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.780
And then I'll give you some
examples of the integration

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.210
so that you can
say, wow, Ryan was

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.720
able to do these
things in Power BI.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.790
Based off of how

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.620
 he's using the functionality,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.260
maybe I can do
these other things

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.560
that Ryan hasn't thought of.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.070
And then lastly, lastly, I will
show you how to get started.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.310
So first, let's address
some of the disconnect.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.150
There are a lot of
unknown unknowns

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.390
that R developers
have about Power BI

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.575
and that Power BI
developers have about R.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.170
From our standpoint, when R
developers look at Power BI,

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.850
they just see another GUI
based data visualization tool.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.920
Power BI is much more than that.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.020
And in this talk, I will show
you the reason behind that.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.690
And then on the
Power BI side,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.190
when property
developers look at R,

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:37.450
they see a domain
specific programming language

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.690
that they think is just for data
scientists and statisticians.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.650
They don't realize that a lot
of the things that Power BI attempts

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.740
to do have been done in
the R community for some time

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.320
now, since the
nineties, I think.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.040
R was developed in
the early 90s, by 93.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.780
So the R community has been
doing things from a data

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.260
visualization standpoint

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.160
That the Power BI community

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.660
has recently started
doing so we will address that.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.455
Well, that's on the other side.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:09.080
When I'm doing
different types of talk,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.190
I try to tell the
Power BI community

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.240
some of the things that
you can do using that tool

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.430
that they're not aware of.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.310
So now that I got that
address, the disconnect,

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.500
let me introduce Power BI to
some of you that are new to it

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.090
from a high level.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.280
Power BI is a data
visualization tool.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.410
So in order to do data
visualization,

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.610
you need data.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.040
And the data model
that Power BI  prefers

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.860
is a data model known as
the star schema data model.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.350
The star schema data
model is made up of

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:51.250
two types of tables: a dimension
table and a fact table.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.690
The fact table you see here in
the center and the fact table

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:55.705
stores measures.

00:04:55.705 --> 00:04:57.330
You can think of a
measure as something

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.270
that measures a
business activity.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.540
And this particular
simple example,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.710
we see that the measures
are price and quantity.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.070
And then the fact
table is connected

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.520
to the dimension table.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.980
And then the dimension
tables you have

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.050
dimensions grouped by category.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.000
So you see in this
particular example,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.920
we have a store dimension,
a employee dimension

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:26.880
and so on and so forth.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.580
You can read the screen and
those different categories.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:35.580
You have dimensions that
you can group your measures

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.800
and your fact table by.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.480
Another thing I
want to point out

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.830
about Power BI from a data
standpoint is how the data is

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.770
stored in Power BI.
Power BI uses an engine

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.430
called a VertiPaq engine.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.000
And the take away
I want you to get

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.420
from that is that
the VertiPaq engine

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.500
one, stores a data memory using
what is called column store

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.030
indexing.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.160
And the takeaway
you should get is

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.430
that storing data in that
fashion does two things.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.530
It makes performance, you
know, some complex calculation

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:15.310
relatively faster than you
will then what it will perform

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:16.870
if you're using another tool.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:20.730
And in addition to that, the
column store indexing increases

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:24.810
the memory footprint
of your data by a lot,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.110
in some cases up to a tenth of
the size of the original data

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.640
set.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.060
So the second thing I want to
point out is the built-in data

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.420
wrangling tool that's in Power BI.
This built in data

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:38.490
wrangling tool,

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:40.720
if you are using
Microsoft Excel,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.310
this tool is built
into that as well.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:47.010
This is like a gym that many
people don't know about.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:53.110
This tool is called Power Query,
and this tool, using a GUI,

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.400
allows you to do many
types of data wrangling

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.400
tasks with low code.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.520
Super easy.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.850
A person who has no
coding experience

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.100
can do a lot of
complex data wrangling

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.770
that we in the R
community will probably

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:13.060
have to write a dplyr
script to do with no code.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:15.790
And then in the instances
where you need to use code,

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:19.360
it has a programming language
underneath the hood called M

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.060
that you can write some code
to do some things that you

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.700
can't do throughout the GUI.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:28.090
Super powerful and again is
built in Microsoft Excel,

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.190
so it's a tool you should
look into if you're

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.640
doing any type of data wrangling
in Microsoft Excel as well,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.680
and this is what the
interface looks like,

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.510
and you'll see that
the interface is

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:40.700
similar to some of the
other Office products.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:44.560
So the learning
curve with getting up

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.240
to speed with this particular
tool is pretty shallow.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:54.550
The feature that I the
best about Power BI

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.650
are the calculated measures.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.500
What I like
about calculated measures

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.180
is the way they operate
inside of Power BI.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:05.650
They are what I
call context-based,

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.040
meaning that they calculate
based off of the context

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.190
that you put them in.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:14.500
So in this particular situation,
we have a simple matrix

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:20.110
where we're displaying
three different measures,

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:24.072
and those measures are the sales
amount calculated measures,

00:08:24.072 --> 00:08:25.780
and those are the
sales amount calculated

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.390
measure, the comparison
sales amount

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:32.500
calculated measure, and adjusted
comparison sales amount

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.960
calculated measure.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.820
One thing I want to
point out is that we

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:41.860
look at the highlighted
cell, in that cell,

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:44.530
the comparison sales
amount calculated

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:50.530
measure is calculated in the
context of the events work

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:56.920
brand and also the context
of the comparison date filter

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:02.200
that you see in the slide
filter and on the upper right

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.250
portion of the screen.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:08.190
Notice I didn't
mention the filter that

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.160
was being applied by
the other date slicer

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.210
and I only mentioned
the slicer on the right.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:15.360
That is because the
programming language

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.090
that Power BI uses, a
programming language

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.690
called DAX, Data Analysis
Expression, programming

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:22.310
language.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.890
It allows you to incorporate
some pretty sophisticated

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.820
business logic inside of it.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:29.790
And in this
particular situation,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:36.510
you can see that we were able
to remove the filter from one

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.390
of the slicers and we
only got the filter that

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.230
was applied in the slicers
in the upper right.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:44.500
Super powerful feature.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.870
You will be surprised at
the type of business logic

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:52.290
that you can encapsulate
inside of a DAX expression

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.970
using Power BI.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.010
And then, of course, it's
a data visualization tool.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:00.640
So just like any other
data visualization

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:02.110
tool,
whether you're

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.160
talking about Power
BI, Qlik, Tableau,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.380
I know we have a speaker talking
about that particular tool.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:13.223
You can build dashboards
and get as artsy as you want.

00:10:13.223 --> 00:10:14.640
You know, you can
get real pretty.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.115
I'm not good at that aspect.

00:10:16.115 --> 00:10:17.740
You know, I do
everything pretty bland,

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.290
but if you are a
very colorful person,

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.780
you can do that or
you can pretty much do

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.630
any type of visual you
want and Power BI just

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.930
like you can with any
other visualization tool.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.710
Now up to this point,
you may be saying, Ryan,

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:35.320
you just described a
pretty awesome tool.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.670
Why do we even need the R
integration inside of Power BI?

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:42.010
How do we even
benefit from this?

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.050
Well, this is my opinion.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.670
This little graph right here.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:51.620
This is my opinionated view
about how I think Power BI is.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:53.980
This is my assessment of
Power BI using these four

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:55.220
different categories.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.220
And in these four
different categories,

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.530
you see in most
of the categories,

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:03.730
I think Power BI is
a very awesome tool

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.190
other than the data
science model piece.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:09.020
It does a good job at
data visualization,

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:11.930
data wrangling for the most
part, for most situations.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.230
And then from a context
based calculation

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.170
that I showed you earlier, that
feature right there in

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:18.330
Power BI is awesome.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:22.060
Now in the data science modeling
side of things is very limited

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:26.200
and that you normally would
need the premium capacity

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.530
version of Power BI to
be able to leverage Azure

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:31.360
Machine Learning services.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.390
And you can do some data
modeling there and then

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:37.070
connect to Power BI from there.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:41.410
Or you can use some
prebuilt data models

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.310
in the form of Azure
Cognitive Services.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.010
So from that standpoint,
it still is kind of clunky

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:50.000
and I don't really recommend it.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.660
But when you add R
to the situation,

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:55.780
you'll see that from
a data viz, there be

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:59.770
in 80 to 80%
to 90% of the time,

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:02.470
you have a visual that's
already baked in the Power BI

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:06.610
or that you can get
from the Microsoft Store

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.510
to import into Power BI.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:11.410
But there would be
those edge cases where

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:15.520
if you are used to coding
in ggplot, you can, you know,

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:17.860
create that visual
easily in ggplot,

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.050
but you can't do in
Microsoft. That is what

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.520
that orange is representing.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.410
And then on the
data wrangling side,

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:27.130
there are situations where you
may need a regular expression

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:30.370
to do some type of
upstream manipulation,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:33.520
or you may need to
do something that is

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.780
pretty clunky to do in Power BI

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.910
but you can easily
do it in dplyr

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:43.240
and those situations is
represented by the orange

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:43.790
there.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:45.850
And then on the
data modeling side,

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.360
if you're doing
something in data science

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.280
where you want to
bring in scores data

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:54.910
into your Power BI data
model, if you use them

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.430
in Power BI in a self
service fashion,

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:00.520
you can take those
R scripts that you

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:04.990
have that will
ingest in data scored

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:08.090
and you can take that scored data
and easily adjust in Power BI.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.620
That's why I added that
little orange for the data

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.030
science modeling piece.

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.550
So now let's talk
about some examples.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:20.750
The first example is a very
simple data science machine

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:25.440
learning example,
using a data set that,

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.510
if you were learning any
type of data science,

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.427
you'd probably be familiar
with this data set.

00:13:29.427 --> 00:13:34.350
The Boston Housing data set and
it's a very simple illustration

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:39.420
of how you can take a model that
was saved in an .Rds file on disk,

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:43.110
consume that model, and then
consume the data that you

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:44.740
need for the scoring.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:49.680
And just like you do in any
other situation with R,

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:53.700
you can use that model and the
new data that you want to score,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.310
score it and then
present that data

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.290
to Power BI as the data set.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:03.450
If you had a more
complex workflow,

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:06.760
you all that went to the
tidyverse workshop here,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:11.160
or if you're familiar
with tidymodels,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.690
you can use tidymodels 
if you have

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.500
a more sophisticated
workflow that you need to do

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.910
prior to scoring the data.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.330
And once you score
that data, the caveat

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:29.600
is that data set needs to
be exposed as a data frame.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.820
If this is exposed
as a data frame

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.650
that you can bring in Power BI.
If you have it in any other data

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:41.570
structure or list, Power BI
would not be able to read it.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:44.900
Another aspect that I
like when using the R

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:47.060
integration in
Power BI is if I need

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:49.350
to read a data from a data API.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:51.920
In this particular
example, I'm reading data

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.070
from the US Census.

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:59.570
And I can easily consume data
from a data API using an R

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.470
from a Power Query standpoint.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.770
They have some integration,
but it's very costly

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:06.630
and it's not as
performance as using R.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.250
So in this situation,

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:12.560
I'm looking at, I'm
integrating the Census API,

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:16.640
bringing in data, and exposing
that data to Power BI,

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.970
and you'll see that it will
be exposed to Power BI as a dataset.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:23.270
That can be extremely
valuable when you're using

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.600
outside data to bring
more value to the data

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.600
that you have in house.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:33.310
Now, the thing I the best is
the data visualization piece

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.680
in terms of the R integration
and this example,

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.320
this visual is near to me.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.700
This is the visual where I was,

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:44.380
it was the first
visual I built when

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:48.310
the R integration
became available in Power BI

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:53.290
and in this situation you see
that we are able to do things

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:57.610
with R that, we're spoiled,
but the Power BI community

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.340
don't even know of some
of these capabilities.

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:03.220
One of those capabilities
is the ability

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:06.540
to have annotations
inside of your graph.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:08.680
Now, in this particular
graph, if you

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:11.200
were to add up all
the different classes,

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:16.990
you see that the numbers
don't add to the total number

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:19.220
of countries, to 235 countries.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:22.300
And I'm able to add
information in my title

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.770
about why that's the case
for each individual violin

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:28.670
plot that's overlaid
with a box plot.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.420
I'm able to get
information about that,

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:34.000
the number of countries that
was in that particular core

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.020
class and the trade balance
for that particular class,

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.260
those type of annotations in
the Power BI community, they're

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:42.410
not aware of.

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:46.340
And those were one of the things
that make it super powerful.

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:50.800
Another thing is, if you
want to choose another year,

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:52.870
you'll see that I can
easily pick a button

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:56.720
and then that filter will
be propagated to my visual.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:00.340
And if I wanted to look
at this and I say, OK,

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.440
looking over to
the left-hand side

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:05.260
and I see that the
Caribbeans for instance,

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:09.380
we have a positive trade
experience with the Caribbean.

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:12.160
So if you look at
the left-hand side,

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.310
I can drill down further
into that information

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:18.369
and get detailed information
using the Power BI

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:19.160
functionality.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:22.500
So this is our visual.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.670
And if we're on the R
side, we're used

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.400
to be able to be this
expressive with our visuals,

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.390
but not on the Power BI side.

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.930
And you as R developers
should reach out

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:37.670
to some of your people in
the Power BI community and, you know,

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.680
teach them how the R
integration can

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.870
be super beneficial to them.

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:43.670
I gave you some
examples here, but there

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:47.630
are many more examples that if
once you start digging into it,

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:50.040
you'll be able to find
out more examples.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.730
And in your unique
use cases, you

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:58.070
can use also to be creative
with using that R integration

00:17:58.070 --> 00:17:58.920
inside of Power BI.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:04.260
That's the end of
this presentation.

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:07.130
If you want, you can
go to this GitHub site

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:11.570
and I have a little GitHub repo
that gives you some high level

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:15.420
information about leveraging
the R integration inside of Power BI.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:20.030
I appreciate you
all coming to the talk

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:23.990
and listening to me tell you
about this R integration

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:25.740
and I hope you

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:29.120
go out and spread the
love of the R community

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:31.820
to other Power BI
developers and show them

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:33.710
why we love the
great programming

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.790
language of R. Thank you.

